currently wildlife has no value no  economic value to ranchers and as such  the ranchers don't want them around if  it doesn't pay it doesn't stick  many Americans trace the modern  conservation movement back to Theodore  Roosevelt known for his love of the  outdoors and for creating the US Forest  Service in the National Park System  but what if government isn't the only or  the best entity to protect America's  natural wonders we also have a history  in this country of private initiative  right if you go back further to the  Roosevelt spoon and Crockett Club for  example it was a combination of artists  and wealthy people and writers and they  came together and they said hey we care  about conservation and we can do this on  our own and we can invite people from  all over the country to join us the  American prairie reserve is a group that  wants to establish the largest Nature  Reserve in the lower 48 states and it  aims to do so with private funding  people often wonder about how big the  current reserve is now and what its  goals are well the end 3/9 million acres  is 5,000 square miles roughly the size  of the state of Connecticut the group  still has a ways to go before reaching  its goal of more than 3 million acres  stretching across northern Montana but  it's already purchased or leased more  than three hundred thousand acres of  land with individual properties larger  than the entire city of Los Angeles the  group hopes to stitch together these  lands with existing public land to  eventually create a space where plants  and animals can thrive and be enjoyed by  visitors our model is a deliberate blend  of private philanthropy working in  cooperation with the federal government  I think everybody who lives in the  American West particularly in Montana is  thankful that there are these large  swaths of federal public lands that  provide us recreation and all sorts of  amenity benefits the problem we see is  these resources are stressed the federal  government the National Park Service has  a maintenance backlog in the billions of  dollars for example there are just  fundamental limits to what the  government can do in an era of serious  fiscal constraints we think rather than  sitting around and waiting and lobbying  the government to put together these big  ecologically meaningful  landscapes private actors like ourself  can really mobilize and achieve  significant conservation goals using a  slightly different model  there was this big era of park building  in the United States and it ended in the  50s really with Grand Teton National  Park and in my opinion part of the  reason that ended it was it was so  cumbersome and there was conflict one  consistent area of conflict that  national parks face is one with ranchers  and hunters who rely on the land for  food and money we've seen it play out  with the Bundy ranch confrontation in  Nevada and more recently with the  occupation of a ranger station in Oregon  most national parks in the US and Beyond  have had these battles that you've  probably heard about in the news when  wolf or bison cross over the boundary of  the public park into the private  property there's kind of guns aiming in  there's a fundamental problem at Cal  consume grass as well as other wildlife  so you have a competition for resources  currently wildlife has no economic value  to ranchers and as such the ranchers  don't want them around but the American  prairie reserve is testing out a way to  work with ranchers to conserve wildlife  so that everybody wins wild sky is a  brand in a company privately owned  called Montana Prairie Holdings that is  a for-profit out there selling beef  selling about 50,000 pounds a month all  over the United States and the  fundamental goal of starting a  for-profit company is its sustainable  over the longer term we generated 3.3  million last year in sales American  prairie reserve uses the profits from  wild sky beef to pay ranchers to help  them create the soft borders that allow  wildlife to thrive ranchers can plant  native grasses allow prairie dogs to  establish colonies and create gaps in  their fences to allow herd animals like  deer and elk to pass through the more  goal posts they pass and the more  wildlife they observe on their property  the bigger the payout is  the fundamental purpose is to change the  social dynamic the sociology of the area  the way people think about wildlife  something different than the way they've  thought about it in the past we've seen  prairie dog towns increasing we've seen  for the first time on one ranch a black  bear he's also some mountain lion on his  property never seen that so we're making  some good steps if American prairie  reserve succeeds it will be one and a  half times the size of Yellowstone  National Park and they believe that a  success that big will provide a lesson  for other western states the approach  that we're employing is unique only in  the sense that we're actually doing it  the conservationists with a market  orientation I've been thinking and  writing about this kind of thing for  decades and the Dean much of this  intellectual work was done here in  Bozeman but we're actually applying this  model on the ground as we're successful  and we gain more attention over time  you'll see other groups trying to put  this together on a much larger scale I  think that's really the important thing  is the scale at which were working
 

